WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.825
Fire extinguishers go by boat length, and the exam loves the table.

2
00:00:05.075 --> 00:00:08.150
Assume no fixed system in the engine room.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.325
Under twenty-six feet: one B-one.

4
00:00:11.575 --> 00:00:15.950
Twenty-six to under forty feet: two B-ones, or one B-two.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.400
Forty to sixty-five feet: three B-ones, or one B-two plus one B-one.

6
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.300
See the pattern?

7
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.150
One, two, three, and a B-two is worth two B-ones.

8
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.550
If you have an approved fixed system in the engine room, you need one fewer B-one.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.625
And disposable extinguishers expire.

10
00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:39.750
Replace them twelve years after the date of manufacture.

11
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.650
One labeling note.

12
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:50.100
Newer extinguishers are marked five-B and twenty-B instead of B-one and B-two, and newer boats must carry those.

13
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.000
Mount them where you can grab one without reaching over the thing that’s likely to be on fire.
